# DEL-04-01 VER-007, the sparse finish's constructed system: a sparse solve, under natural order
# and the first-nonzero profile, that does not meet REQ-014's limit within the kernel's
# refinement policy.
# Binary64 values as f64::to_bits in hex: the line "n N", then the N rows of K, then "f ..."
# (the load) and "x ..." (the solution the load was built from).
#
# Construction (core/solver/frame_kernel/tests/residual_refusal_witnesses.rs regenerates it and
# asserts every bit; no product code is needed given t's bits):
#   splitmix64, state s: s += 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15; z = s; z = (z ^ (z >> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9;
#     z = (z ^ (z >> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EB; next = z ^ (z >> 31)   (wrapping 64-bit)
#   Arrowhead, n = 64: hub DOF 0 joined to leaf j = 1..63 by a spring 2^(next % 21); then each
#     leaf grounded by (1 + next % 7) / t; the hub grounded by 1 / t. Generator seeded
#     11000 + 977*1 + 64. K[0][0] = 1/t, then += each spring in leaf order;
#     K[j][j] = spring_j + ground_j; K[0][j] = K[j][0] = -spring_j; every other entry 0.
#   t = f64::from_bits(0x41b9a3c9f5983550), about 2^28.680313. It was placed by bisecting the ground knob to
#     the kernel's condition-gate boundary for this structure (2^32.876432825, admitted below it) and
#     stepping down 12/19 of two decades. Only this placement used kernel code.
#   x: generator seeded 12000 + 1 + 7*12 + 40; x[i] = 2^40 for odd i (the leaves
#     1, 3, ...), else 2^(next % 4); each times a sign (next % 2 == 0 -> +1, else -1); drawn per
#     entry in that order, the exponent draw skipped for the 2^40 entries.
#   f[i] = sum over j of K[i][j] * x[j], left to right in binary64.
#
# What it shows, and what it does not:
#   - sparse finish, natural order, first-nonzero profile (the kernel's caller-supplied-ordering
#     entry): no result and the named finding "original-equation residual failed after bounded
#     refinement". The order matters: it puts the hub first, the factor fills completely, and the
#     fill is what produces the refusal. The refinement stopped on non-improvement (worst row 3.83
#     of the limit at the first solve, 9.65 after one correction), not on exhausted attempts; the
#     kernel does not expose which, and this was read from an instrumented scratch build.
#   - the same system under the product's ordering (sparse_direct's reverse Cuthill-McKee, both
#     entries) returns a result marked sensitive meeting REQ-014 row by row at the first solve;
#     the dense finish returns one marked sensitive after one refinement. This solve does not meet
#     the limit within the kernel's refinement policy; the system is not one that cannot.
n 64
